package com.bqsqwad.heartbreakhealer.feature.entertainment

/** Original ten-part mystery about a community greenhouse and a set of unsigned seed records. */
internal object StoryLibraryBatch43 {
    val stories = listOf(
        StorySeries(
            id = "the_greenhouse_of_second_chances",
            title = "The Greenhouse of Second Chances",
            tagline = "A student team restores a neglected greenhouse and discovers why its oldest plants were never labelled.",
            parts = listOf(
                StoryPart(1, "The Locked Glass Door", listOf(
                    "The greenhouse behind Westfield School had been locked for three years. Its glass panels were dusty, the path was crowded with weeds, and a handwritten notice warned students not to enter without a staff member. When Imani joined the environment club, she asked why the school had a greenhouse if nobody used it. Their teacher, Mr Yusuf, explained that a storm had damaged the roof and the club had moved its activities elsewhere. Repairs were now complete, but the school had not decided what to grow there.",
                    "During a supervised inspection, Imani noticed a row of healthy green leaves behind the empty seed trays. The plants were growing in old containers, each marked with a number instead of a name. Some numbers appeared twice, and others were missing. The caretaker said the containers had been moved during the repairs. He had watered the plants when he could, but he did not know what they were or who had planted them.",
                    "Imani photographed the labels for the club record and asked Mr Yusuf whether they could identify the plants before changing anything. He agreed, provided they did not taste unknown leaves or move the containers without guidance. Her teammate Chidi found a faded sign on the greenhouse wall: SECOND CHANCES GROW HERE. Beneath it was a date from twelve years earlier. The club decided to find out what the greenhouse had once been used for."
                )),
                StoryPart(2, "Numbers Instead of Names", listOf(
                    "The environment club searched the school's public project archive. They found a report about a student gardening programme that had grown seedlings for the neighbourhood tree-planting group. The report listed numbered trays, but its plant-name column was blank. A note said the original labels had been written in a separate notebook that was missing when the greenhouse closed. The final page thanked a volunteer coordinator named Mrs Nwosu and mentioned that the plants were chosen for local conditions.",
                    "Chidi noticed that the numbers on the containers followed a pattern. Trays one to six were grouped near the water tank, while seven to twelve stood beside the southern window. The report described the same arrangement. Imani wondered whether the numbers matched the original planting order. They copied the layout and compared it with a diagram in the archive. The positions matched, but there was still no reliable way to identify each plant from its number alone.",
                    "Mr Yusuf contacted the former coordinator through the school's alumni office. Mrs Nwosu replied that she remembered the greenhouse, although she could not identify every plant from memory. She offered to visit and help the students search the project records she still had. Until then, she advised them to leave the plants undisturbed. Imani added a note to the club board: first learn what is growing, then decide what the greenhouse needs."
                )),
                StoryPart(3, "The Volunteer Coordinator", listOf(
                    "Mrs Nwosu arrived with a folder of photocopied meeting notes and a small notebook. She explained that the programme had grown seedlings for public spaces, but it had also given students a chance to learn patience and record keeping. Every tray had a number because several seedlings looked alike when they first emerged. The names were written in a separate ledger so the labels could be replaced as the plants grew. The ledger had been kept in the school office during the roof repairs.",
                    "The office transfer list showed that the ledger had been moved to the district education resource room with other project documents. Mrs Nwosu remembered that the final greenhouse team had been preparing a display about native plants and water conservation. She also remembered a disagreement about whether to sell extra seedlings at a school fair or donate them to the community group. The committee had postponed the decision until it could confirm the number of healthy plants.",
                    "Imani asked whether the disagreement explained why the greenhouse had closed. Mrs Nwosu said it did not. The storm had caused the damage, and the committee had simply run out of time and volunteers to restart. She encouraged the students to avoid turning a practical delay into a story about blame. The club thanked her and arranged to visit the resource room with Mr Yusuf. Their next clue was a ledger that might still contain the missing plant names."
                )),
                StoryPart(4, "The Ledger in Storage", listOf(
                    "The resource-room archivist found the greenhouse ledger in a box of school environmental projects. Its cover was stained, but the pages were intact. The first section listed the tray numbers and plant names. The second section recorded watering dates, growth notes, and the intended destination of each batch. Several plants were marked for the school courtyard, while others were reserved for a community centre that had planned a small shaded garden.",
                    "The last pages were more difficult to understand. They contained a series of crossed-out entries and a note that some seedlings had been kept back because their roots needed more time to develop. A separate sheet listed the names of students who had volunteered to care for them during the school break. Imani recognised one family name from the current school board, but Mr Yusuf reminded her that a shared surname did not prove a connection. They would ask through the official alumni contact rather than making assumptions.",
                    "The archivist allowed them to scan the relevant pages for the school project. Imani and Chidi returned to the greenhouse and matched the first six containers with the ledger. The labels were faded, so they made new temporary tags with the plant names and source references. They left the remaining containers untouched until Mrs Nwosu could help verify them. The greenhouse was beginning to look less like a forgotten room and more like a classroom with a history."
                )),
                StoryPart(5, "The Plants That Stayed", listOf(
                    "Mrs Nwosu examined the remaining containers and identified several seedlings from the old programme. A few had grown beyond their original trays, and one had been divided during the roof repairs. The ledger explained that these plants were meant to stay in the greenhouse until they were strong enough to move outdoors. The planned community-centre garden had been delayed, so the seedlings had remained in their containers longer than expected. The caretaker had kept them alive without knowing their full story.",
                    "The students asked the community centre whether it still wanted a garden. Its manager said the old plan had changed because the courtyard was now used for other activities. However, a nearby reading room had a small outdoor space that could support shade plants and a few raised containers. The manager invited the school to submit a proposal, but warned that the space had to remain clear for visitors and wheelchair access.",
                    "Imani proposed that the club grow only the plants suited to the reading room and keep the others for the school courtyard. Chidi suggested adding a simple care sheet so future volunteers would know how much water each plant needed. Mr Yusuf approved the planning work but asked them to consult the reading-room staff before choosing a layout. The plants had waited long enough; the students wanted their next home to be one where they could thrive."
                )),
                StoryPart(6, "A Garden Designed Together", listOf(
                    "The club visited the reading room with its manager and measured the outdoor space. They marked the doorway, walking path, seating area, and drainage point on a simple plan. The manager asked them to keep tall containers away from the windows and leave enough room for visitors to turn comfortably. Imani adjusted the design and placed the proposed plants along the sunny wall. The group also included a low table where visitors could read about the plants without needing to bend down.",
                    "They presented the plan to the reading-room committee. One member suggested adding herbs, but Mrs Nwosu explained that the original seedlings had been selected for shade and local conditions, not for cooking. The club decided to keep the first stage simple and ask visitors for suggestions after the garden had settled. They would not promise food plants or medicinal benefits without checking suitability and safety.",
                    "The committee approved a small pilot with clear responsibilities. The school would supply the labelled plants and a care guide; the reading room would provide access to water and notify staff if a container needed attention. The students would visit with a teacher during agreed hours. Imani wrote every responsibility on the plan. A successful garden, she realised, depended as much on reliable care as on good soil."
                )),
                StoryPart(7, "The Roof and the Rain", listOf(
                    "Before moving the plants, the club inspected the greenhouse roof with the maintenance team. The repaired panels were secure, but a gutter had become blocked by leaves. The maintenance staff cleared it and showed the students how to report future problems. Mr Yusuf explained that students should not climb or attempt repairs themselves. Their role was to notice, record, and ask the right people for help.",
                    "A heavy shower arrived during the next club meeting. Water collected near the greenhouse door, and the team discovered that one drainage channel was still slow. They moved the plant trays away from the damp area under staff supervision and reported the issue. The maintenance team corrected the slope and tested the drain. The delay gave the club time to finish the care sheets and check the plant names against the ledger one more time.",
                    "The caretaker brought out the old sign that had hung above the door. The words SECOND CHANCES GROW HERE were almost hidden beneath dust. The club cleaned the sign carefully and asked the school whether it could be displayed again. The head teacher agreed, adding that the phrase could describe the greenhouse's renewal as well as the seedlings' long wait. Imani felt that the project was finally moving at a pace that respected both the plants and the people caring for them."
                )),
                StoryPart(8, "The First Planting Day", listOf(
                    "On planting day, the club arrived at the reading room with the approved containers, labels, and care guide. Staff showed them the prepared space and checked the layout against the accessibility plan. The students placed each container where the manager had marked it. They used the ledger to record the plant names and the date of transfer. No one rushed to fill every corner; the committee had agreed that open space was part of the design.",
                    "A young visitor asked why some plants had numbers on their old labels. Imani explained that the numbers came from the original greenhouse system and that the new labels included the names so people could learn about them. The visitor chose a favourite leaf shape and asked whether the club would return to see how the plants grew. Chidi said they had planned monthly visits, but the schedule would depend on the reading room's needs and the school's calendar.",
                    "At the end of the afternoon, the manager thanked the students and asked for a copy of the care guide. The club left one in a clear folder near the staff desk, not beside the plants where it might be damaged. On the way back, Mrs Nwosu said the old programme would have been pleased to see the seedlings in a place where people could enjoy them. Imani looked at the empty spaces on the plan. They were not unfinished gaps. They were room for the garden to grow."
                )),
                StoryPart(9, "The Record of Every Leaf", listOf(
                    "The club created a permanent greenhouse record with photographs, dates, plant names, and notes about each transfer. They included the original ledger reference and explained which details had been confirmed by Mrs Nwosu. A few plants remained at school because the reading room did not have suitable light for them. The students labelled those containers and added a weekly check to the club calendar, with a teacher responsible for confirming that the task was covered during holidays.",
                    "The reading room sent a message after the first month. The plants were healthy, but one container needed to be moved because it narrowed the path when visitors gathered for an event. The manager suggested a new position, and the club reviewed it with the accessibility adviser before making the change. They updated the layout map so the next group would not repeat the problem.",
                    "The school archive added the new record beside the original greenhouse report. It now showed the full journey: planting, delay, roof repairs, identification, and transfer. Imani insisted that the caretaker's contribution be included. He had kept the plants alive when the project was paused, even without knowing their names. The record recognised that care can be important even when nobody is keeping a list of who provided it."
                )),
                StoryPart(10, "The Greenhouse of Second Chances", listOf(
                    "At the end of term, the environment club held an open afternoon in the greenhouse. Visitors could read the plant labels, view the old ledger scan, and see the new garden plan. Mrs Nwosu spoke about the original project, while the caretaker explained how the roof repairs had been completed. The students showed the care guide and described how they had worked with the reading room rather than deciding everything on its behalf.",
                    "The head teacher announced that the greenhouse would reopen as a supervised learning space. Future clubs could grow seedlings, study local plants, and plan small community projects. The school would keep the transfer records and review the care schedule each term. The reading room agreed to continue the garden pilot for another season, with changes made only after staff and visitors had a chance to give feedback.",
                    "Imani placed the restored sign above the greenhouse door. The words looked simple, but she now understood how much work they contained. The plants had survived because someone kept watering them, the records had been found because someone kept them safe, and the garden had succeeded because people listened before acting. The greenhouse was no longer locked. It was a place where unfinished plans could be examined, improved, and given another chance to grow."
                ))
            )
        )
    )
}
